WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.265
Access to Information Processing Crisis.

00:00:03.215 --> 00:00:08.386
The Access to Information Act gives Canadians the right to request government documents.

00:00:08.386 --> 00:00:11.454
The statutory deadline is thirty days.

00:00:11.454 --> 00:00:17.534
The reality: months to years of processing delays, routine extensions, and heavy redactions.

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:23.045
The Information Commissioner has repeatedly flagged the system as failing its democratic purpose.

00:00:23.045 --> 00:00:28.738
The transparency mechanism designed to hold government accountable has become a barrier to accountability.

00:00:28.738 --> 00:00:30.579
The ATIP system.

00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:37.249
The Access to Information Act requires government institutions to respond to requests within thirty calendar days.

00:00:37.249 --> 00:00:42.693
Extensions are permitted for consultations, large volumes, or third-party notifications.

00:00:42.693 --> 00:00:46.988
In practice, extensions are routine and often measured in months.

00:00:46.988 --> 00:00:50.249
Some departments have backlogs exceeding one year.

00:00:50.249 --> 00:00:54.340
The Information Commissioner has described the system as in crisis.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.624
The transparency blockade.

00:00:56.624 --> 00:01:03.977
The PHAC mandate fraud was exposed through ATIP documents showing the travel ban had limited epidemiological justification.

00:01:03.977 --> 00:01:09.420
The SNC-Lavalin scandal relied on cabinet confidence waivers to allow partial testimony.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.545
ArriveCAN procurement failures were documented through ATIP releases.

00:01:13.545 --> 00:01:16.068
The system works — when it works.

00:01:16.068 --> 00:01:24.079
But the processing delays, routine extensions, and heavy redactions mean that by the time documents are released, the political moment has passed.

00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:28.318
Delayed transparency is functionally equivalent to no transparency.
